      "latePostId": "69e73bb56013b43447173ab2",
      "content": "A single ChatGPT query uses 10 times the electricity of a Google search — and the math is reviving nuclear power plants 🤖⚡\n\nThe AI Energy Problem:\n\nThe comparison:\n→ Google search: ~0.3 watt-hours\n→ ChatGPT query: ~2.9 watt-hours (~10x)\n→ Image generation: even higher\n→ AI training runs: can consume as much electricity as a small city for weeks\n\nWhy AI uses so much power:\n→ GPU clusters running 24/7 (can't turn off or warm-up is too slow)\n→ Inference (serving responses) is always-on\n→ Data center cooling: 30-40% of power goes to cooling the hardware\n→ Scale: billions of queries daily\n\nThe infrastructure math:\n→ US data centers: currently ~1-2% of US electricity consumption\n→ Projected: double by 2030, driven specifically by AI workloads\n→ IEA: global data center electricity could reach 1,000 TWh/year by 2026 (equal to Japan's total consumption)\n→ Grid can't expand renewable capacity fast enough for this rate of growth\n\nWhy nuclear:\n→ 24/7 baseload power (unlike solar/wind, which are intermittent)\n→ Carbon-free\n→ Predictable output regardless of weather\n→ Long-term contracts possible\n\nThe deals:\n→ Microsoft: 20-year PPA to restart Three Mile Island Unit 1 (Constellation Energy, 2023)\n→ The other unit ran until 2019; Unit 1 was unaffected by the 1979 accident\n→ Google: deal with Kairos Power for small modular reactors (SMRs)\n→ Amazon: investing in multiple nuclear startups\n→ Meta: seeking nuclear power bids for data centers\n→ Oracle: planning campus powered entirely by nuclear\n\nThe geopolitical dimension:\n→ Countries with reliable nuclear baseload = structural AI capacity advantage\n→ US nuclear capacity: declining for decades, now reversing\n→ China building 150+ new nuclear reactors\n\n#aifacts #nuclear #datacenter #energy #artificialintelligence #threemileisland #techenergy #climatetech #futureofai #aiinfrastructure",
