      "_id": "69ea49347dea335c2b2cd469",
      "latePostId": "69e88eb71505b84f323fceb0",
      "content": "AI language models don't read words — they read fragments. And the language you write in affects how expensive every query is 🤖\n\nHow Tokenization Works:\n\nWhat a token is:\n→ Not a word — a chunk of text (~2-4 characters on average)\n→ Common words are one token: 'the', 'is', 'at'\n→ Less common words split into pieces: 'ham' + 'bur' + 'ger' = 3 tokens\n→ 'ChatGPT' = 2-3 tokens\n→ 'Tokenization' = 4 tokens\n→ Punctuation and spaces are separate tokens\n\nWhy it matters:\n→ Context window = measured in tokens, not words\n→ GPT-4's 128K context window = ~100K English words\n→ API pricing = per token (input + output)\n→ Your cost depends on token count, not word count\n→ Rule of thumb: 1 token ≈ 0.75 English words\n\nThe language inequality:\nTokenizers are trained primarily on English text — so English is most efficient:\n→ English: 1x baseline\n→ Spanish / French: ~1.3x more tokens for same content\n→ Arabic: ~2-3x more tokens\n→ Chinese / Japanese: ~4-5x more tokens\n→ A 15-token English question may be 50+ tokens in Japanese\n\nWhat this means practically:\n→ Non-English users: pay more per word\n→ Non-English users: hit context limits sooner with equivalent content\n→ Non-English users: get less effective model capacity per dollar\n→ Same model, same task, different cost based purely on language\n\nThe equity issue:\n→ Flagged by researchers as structural disadvantage\n→ Affects billions of non-English speakers\n→ OpenAI has acknowledged the problem but tokenizer changes are complex\n\nWhat's improving:\n→ Llama 3 tokenizer: ~4x improvement in non-English efficiency vs Llama 2\n→ Gemini tokenizers: designed with multilingual efficiency from start\n→ Each generation of models narrows but doesn't close the gap\n\n#aifacts #tokenization #llm #chatgpt #aiexplained #artificialintelligence #howaiworks #languageai #aiinequality #techeducation",
