    {
      "_id": "69ea965b7dea335c2b2fb286",
      "latePostId": "69e88ec34bbb78b0d058e65c",
      "content": "In February 2023, Meta released their frontier AI model to the public — and the internet ran with it in ways no one expected 🤖\n\nThe Llama Timeline:\n\nFeb 2023: Llama 1 released:\n→ Meta: 'for researchers only'\n→ Reality: weights leaked to 4chan within days\n→ Within 1 week: quantized to run on consumer laptops\n→ Georgi Gerganov's llama.cpp: ran 7B model on MacBook Air\n→ Within 1 month: 100s of fine-tuned variants on HuggingFace\n→ Jailbreaks that were impossible via ChatGPT API: trivial to implement\n\nJuly 2023: Llama 2:\n→ Broader commercial license\n→ Usable for products with <700M monthly active users\n→ Immediately: second wave of fine-tuning, commercial applications\n\nApril 2024: Llama 3:\n→ 8B and 70B parameter models\n→ 8B model: competitive with GPT-3.5 on most benchmarks\n→ 70B model: approaching GPT-4 on multiple benchmarks\n→ Runs on MacBook Pro M3 with 16GB RAM at usable speed\n→ A 2020 supercomputer-level capability on a laptop\n\nThe domino effect:\n→ DeepSeek (China) open-sourced R1: matched GPT-4o performance\n→ Training cost reported: ~$6 million vs GPT-4's ~$100M+\n→ NVIDIA stock dropped 17% in one day on the news\n→ Mistral, Falcon, Phi: all followed open-source model\n\nWhat this broke:\n→ 'You need enormous resources to build frontier AI' — false\n→ 'Closed weights are necessary for safety' — debated\n→ 'US companies have permanent edge' — uncertain\n\nWho now runs local AI:\n→ Researchers in countries without US API access\n→ Companies with data privacy requirements\n→ Privacy-conscious individuals\n→ Developers building offline AI applications\n\n#aifacts #llama #opensource #meta #aiindustry #artificialintelligence #deepseek #openaiai #techindustry #aihistory",
